-- Migration 0038: correct soccer_news team tagging + backfill match_id.
-- Forward-only, two concerns kept in one file because the second is a direct
-- consequence of the first (see below) and both are one-time data
-- corrections that ship alongside the code fix in
-- src/lib/signals/news/soccer/rss.ts.
--
-- THE BUG (docs/handoffs/news-tagging-and-grading-gap_2026-09-16.md has the
-- full writeup): tagMentions() matched a club on a bare whole-word alias hit
-- with no further check. Several clubs' aliases are also ordinary English
-- words, city names, or the generic half of some OTHER real club's name —
-- "Sporting" (Sporting CP vs. Sporting Kansas City), "Inter" (Internazionale
-- vs. Inter Miami), "City" (Manchester City vs. Orlando City / Kansas City),
-- "United" (Manchester United vs. Newcastle/West Ham/Leeds United), "Madrid"
-- (Real Madrid vs. any other Madrid-based club, chiefly Atlético Madrid, a
-- genuine UEFA Champions League entrant this season). A Guardian MLS
-- roundup mentioning Sporting KC, Orlando City and Inter Miami in one
-- paragraph tagged Sporting CP + Manchester City + Internazionale, all
-- three wrong, which is what surfaced this on the Lens v Sporting CP match
-- page. match_id was null on nearly every row, so the precise per-match tier
-- had nothing to fall back on.
--
-- THE FIX (code, not this file): src/lib/signals/news/soccer/rss.ts now
-- treats sporting/inter/city/united/athletic/dynamo/rangers/madrid as
-- "weak" aliases that only tag a club when corroborated by a real fixture
-- (soccer_matches) between it and another club the same story confidently
-- names, within 10 days of publish. The same fixture data now also
-- opportunistically resolves match_id when exactly two clubs are confirmed
-- and exactly one match between them falls within 4 days of publish —
-- never guessing between two candidates.
--
-- THIS FILE reruns that exact corrected logic (normalizeTeamName's
-- diacritic + Turkish dotless-ı folding included, ported faithfully —
-- confirmed against team-match.ts's documented rule that an alias value
-- must never itself be an alias key, which does not apply here since no
-- alias table changed) against every live-competition soccer_news row
-- (fifa.world is archived and explicitly excluded throughout; is_engine_take
-- rows are excluded too — the engine-take path always sets team_ids to the
-- real match's own two teams and never goes through this tagger) and:
--
--  1. Removes a team_id ONLY where the recompute proves it was an
--     uncorroborated hit on one of the ambiguous words above — 163 such
--     (row, team_id) pairs across 156 rows, measured 2026-09-16. It
--     deliberately does NOT remove a team_id the recompute simply "can't
--     find" in the stored text for any other reason: soccer_news.summary is
--     a 3-sentence trim of the original RSS description (trimToSentences in
--     rss.ts), not the full text the original tagger ran against, so a
--     team mentioned only in the trimmed-off tail would look like a miss
--     here even though the original tag was correct. Conflating that with a
--     real ambiguous-alias miss would strip legitimate tags — "when unsure,
--     leave the row alone" means every id outside these 163 explicit pairs
--     is untouched, no matter how the recompute scores it.
--     array_remove() is a no-op on an id that isn't present, so this half
--     is naturally re-runnable.
--
--  2. Backfills match_id for 546 rows that are currently null, using only
--     the corrected (post-removal) team_ids and the same "exactly two
--     confirmed clubs, exactly one fixture within 4 days" rule the code
--     now applies going forward. Guarded by "match_id is null" so it never
--     overwrites a value a later run already set, and can be re-run safely.
--
-- Both updates are plain UPDATE ... FROM (VALUES ...) joins — no dynamic
-- logic lives in SQL; every row here is a literal, already-computed
-- correction, not a rule the database re-derives.

-- 1. Strip uncorroborated ambiguous-alias tags (live competitions only).
